## 2. Social Engineering Techniques & Attack Vectors

| Attack Technique | Definition & Characteristics |
|---|---|
| **Phishing** | Fraudulent acquisition of credentials, PII, or financial data via deceptive digital communications. |
| **Smishing** | Phishing conducted via SMS text messaging. |
| **Vishing** | Phishing conducted over voice calls, interactive voice response (IVR), or voice mail. |
| **Whaling** | High-targeted spear phishing aimed at senior executives (C-suite, board members) or high-net-worth individuals. |
| **Pretexting** | Fabricating an elaborate fictional scenario or identity to justify requesting unauthorized access or information. |
| **Business Email Compromise (BEC)** | Hijacking or spoofing legitimate corporate email accounts to execute wire-transfer fraud or sensitive data transfer. |
| **Brand Impersonation** | Mimicking recognizable corporate brands (banks, shipping services, IT platforms) to trick users into clicking malicious links. |
| **Typosquatting** | Registering common typographical errors of popular domain names (e.g., `exampel.com`) to deliver exploits or fake login forms. |
| **Watering Hole Attack** | Compromising a legitimate third-party website frequently visited by a specific targeted group or organization. |

